[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":157},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":-7,"_18":19,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25},"tipo","metodo","cubo",{"_11":155,"_13":27,"_28":156},"id","sarah-advanced","nome","Sarah's Advanced","nomeLongo","1st layer and the rest in one go","termo","niveis",[47],"introducao","<p>The step after Sarah&#39;s Intermediate: after the 1st layer, the top face and the centers are solved together, in a single algorithm. There are many more cases, but almost all are made of <a href=\"/en/glossary#sledgehammer\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Two triggers: the sledgehammer is R' F R F' and the hedgeslammer, F R' F' R. On the Skewb, they make up almost every algorithm in Sarah's…\">sledgehammers</a> and hedgeslammers, so you can learn them in parts.</p>\n","etapas",[32,33],"historias",[26],{"_13":27,"_28":29,"_30":31},"Skewb","endereco","/historia#skewb","resumo","The Skewb was invented by Tony Durham and released by Uwe Mèffert around 1982, first as the \"Pyraminx Cube\"; the name Skewb came from Douglas Hofstadter, in his column about the Rubik's Cube.",{"_11":148,"_13":149,"_36":150,"_38":151,"_40":152,"_41":-7,"_43":153},{"_11":34,"_13":35,"_36":37,"_38":39,"_40":-7,"_41":42,"_43":44},"l2l","The rest in one go (L2L)","html","<p>Recognize the case by the top face (&quot;Pi&quot; and &quot;Peanut&quot; are the shapes the yellow makes) and by the centers, and do the algorithm. Start with the subgroups with fewer cases and use Intermediate for the ones you don&#39;t know yet.</p>\n","grupos",[45],"pagina","treino","/skewb/treino?grupo=advanced","imagem","/imagens/diagramas/994328254fbd22e6.svg",{"_11":14,"_13":14,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_28":54},"nivel","avancado","casos",93,"conjunto",false,"ids",[55,56,57,58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68,69,70,71,72,73,74,75,76,77,78,79,80,81,82,83,84,85,86,87,88,89,90,91,92,93,94,95,96,97,98,99,100,101,102,103,104,105,106,107,108,109,110,111,1